cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use psmc-rs, please cite it using the metadata below, together with the original PSMC paper listed under references."
title: "psmc-rs: a Rust implementation of the Pairwise Sequentially Markovian Coalescent (PSMC)"
type: software
authors:
  # TODO(author): add `given-names` and `family-names`; they are deliberately
  # left out rather than guessed.
  # TODO(author): add `orcid: "https://orcid.org/XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX"`.
  - alias: larryivanhan
repository-code: "https://github.com/larryivan/psmc-rs"
license: MIT
# The version of Cargo.toml. TODO(release): add `date-released` when v0.2.0
# is tagged, and a `doi` once the software is archived (e.g. on Zenodo).
version: "0.2.0"
abstract: >-
  psmc-rs reimplements Heng Li's PSMC (Pairwise Sequentially Markovian
  Coalescent) in Rust. It infers the history of effective population size
  from a single diploid genome with C psmc's model and defaults, reads
  psmcfa, multihetsep and VCF input (including the vcf2fq | fq2psmcfa route
  in one step), checks inputs before fitting, runs C psmc's bootstrap and
  TMRCA posterior decoding, and writes JSON parameters, an interactive HTML
  report and C psmc's .psmc output. Its results are checked against the
  original C implementation.
keywords:
  - population genetics
  - demographic inference
  - coalescent
  - hidden Markov model
  - PSMC
  - effective population size
  - Rust
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Inference of human population history from individual whole-genome sequences"
    authors:
      - family-names: Li
        given-names: Heng
      - family-names: Durbin
        given-names: Richard
    journal: Nature
    volume: 475
    start: 493
    end: 496
    year: 2011
    doi: 10.1038/nature10231
